 is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{E}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139535476 17592186045443 true] [17592186247114 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 & 9 \\\\ 1 & -9 & -9 \\\\ -1 & 7 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2.$ Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." 13194139535476 17592186045443 false] [17592186247114 1071 "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -3 & 9 \\\\ 1 & -9 & -9 \\\\ -1 & 7 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2.$ Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." 13194139535476 17592186045443 true] [17592186247114 1074 "Notice that we are given a $3\\times 3$ matrix that has precisely $3$ distinct eigenvalues. \n\nTherefore, $A$ is diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\n{J}_{1}(4)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(3)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-2)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks." 13194139535476 17592186045443 false] [17592186247114 1074 "Notice that we are given a $3\\times 3$ matrix that has precisely $3$ distinct eigenvalues. \n\nTherefore, $A$ is diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\n{J}_{1}(4)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(3)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-2)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks." 13194139535476 17592186045443 true] [17592186247115 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 9 & 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=8,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-4.$ Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." 13194139535476 17592186045443 false] [17592186247